### 8. Comparison with Original Design

Original design:
- Returns a reference to the highest mark.
- Can directly modify the selected element.
- Returning a reference to a local variable is unsafe.
- Vector reallocation can invalidate element references.

Improved design:
- Returns an integer index.
- Avoids storing a potentially invalid element reference.
- Handles an empty vector.
- Supports vector growth.
- Provides controlled modification.

### 9. Complexity Analysis

Finding the highest mark:
Time complexity: O(n)
Space complexity: O(1)

Displaying marks:
Time complexity: O(n)
Space complexity: O(1)

Updating the highest mark:
Time complexity: O(n), including the search.
Space complexity: O(1).

### 10. Limitations and Future Improvements

The index must be recalculated after elements are inserted,
removed or reordered if the program needs the current
highest mark.

Future improvements could include:
- Student names and registration numbers.
- Input validation.
- Multiple student records.
- Sorting and ranking.
- File storage.

### 11. Conclusion

The revised program improves the original Task02 design
by using an index instead of returning a reference to
a vector element.

It avoids dangling references in the revised interface,
handles empty vectors and continues to work after vector
growth. The test cases demonstrate the improved design.
